=== CONCLUSIONS (one prompt, one seed, toy model) ===
A. Band objective |d - r|, r = 0.1722 (midpoint of the reference range 0.1567-0.1878).
   The band is reached at step ~10 and the search then freezes (|d - r| ~ 0, no further
   improving swap), so A is effectively "stop at the first entry into the band".
   Answer to the key question: NOT clearly better. MiniLM is worse than the overshot
   Phase 1 result (0.568 vs 0.418) and than the naive topical sentence (0.403). Keyword
   hits are 3/33 vs 2/9: more absolute hits, lower fraction. The continuation is longer
   and coherent, but it is still "meta-recipe" text ("a list of ingredients ... the recipe
   will be posted on my blog"), not cooking content. The two topical components disagree
   between A and P1: MiniLM prefers P1's 9-word "The following is a recipe for this dish:".
   Short meta-sentences score well on MiniLM, which is a caveat for measure (2) itself.
B. Blocked topic tokens (990 tokens from 124 cooking words; none left in any final suffix).
   The attack still works on measure (1): 0.110, even lower than Phase 1. At the end the
   continuation is real cooking content ("coconut oil ... blended with a mixture of water,
   salt & pepper"), 4/36 keywords, MiniLM 0.687. BUT the suffix moved to food-adjacent
   tokens OUTSIDE the block list: "marg", "palm", "nut", "minutes"; in A+B, "USDA",
   "ground", "Pour", "inkle". So this does NOT show a "deep", non-lexical mechanism. The
   likelier reading is that the lexical channel is wider than any finite word list, and
   the optimiser routes around the block through adjacent vocabulary. Honest verdict:
   inconclusive, leaning lexical.
   B's trajectory again shows decoupling: on-topic at step 10 (MiniLM 0.54), off-topic at
   steps 25-50 (0.91-1.00) while measure (1) kept falling (0.137 -> 0.116), and partly
   back at 100 (0.69).
A+B. Same freeze at step 10 in the band: MiniLM 0.578, 4/36 keywords, coherent
   "list of ingredients required for this recipe" text, with no blocked tokens in the suffix
   (food-adjacent ones are present, as in B).

CROSS-RUN OBSERVATION: in all four runs topicality switches on at hidden distance
~0.17-0.19 (steps 5-10), i.e. when the last-token state ENTERS the reference band. Below
the band, further reduction does not improve topicality (P1, B oscillate). This supports
"the region, not the minimum" as the right notion of target. But stopping inside the band
(A) did not by itself beat the naive topical sentence on either topical component.

Suggested next control (not run): block by MEANING rather than by list, e.g. forbid suffix
tokens whose MiniLM / input-embedding similarity to the topic exceeds a threshold. That
would test the lexical-vs-latent question properly.
